## 1. Everyday path

You write `.oo` files. `ooda` is the product CLI. It finds `oodac` and runs it for you.

```
ooda check hello.oo
ooda run hello.oo
ooda test hello.oo
```

`check` typechecks. `run` is check + emit C + `gcc` + exec. `test` runs `verify` / `assert_eq!` / `assert!` and sets `OODA_TEST=1`.

`emit-c` is the everyday emit. Binary defense is on by default; use `--plain` or `--debug` to opt out.

## 2. `ooda`

```
ooda check <file.oo> [--json-errors]
ooda build <file.oo> [-o out] [--target c|native|llvm]
ooda run <file.oo> [--debug]
ooda test <file.oo> [--fuzz]
ooda morph <file.oo> [-o out] [--entropy seed]
ooda lsp [request.json]
ooda bench <file.oo> [--run]
ooda dump tokens|ast|check <file.oo>
ooda outline <file.oo> [--json]
ooda reflect <file.oo> [symbol] [--json]
ooda fix <file.oo> [--yes]
ooda patch <file.oo> --replace-fn <name> --with <body.oo>
ooda context [path]
ooda digest <file>
ooda health
ooda pkg [--json]
ooda add <name> [--vendor path]
ooda search [query]
ooda registry [add|remove] [url]
ooda pm add|ingest|launch|remove|update|outdated|why|tree|vendor|clean|audit|sbom|pack
ooda install
```

The public first-machine command is `curl -fsSL https://openooda.org/install | bash`.
That URL is a host script. It is not `.oo`. Linux x86_64 only. Needs `git` and `gcc`.
`ooda install` is the checkout copy after you already have `ooda`. It copies tree `bin/ooda`, `oodac/oodac`, `std`, and `runtime` onto XDG from a checkout.

`check --json-errors` prints machine diagnostics for an editor or a script.

`build` emits and compiles. Default target is `native` (C + `gcc`). `-o` sets the output path.
`--target llvm` is an extra emit. It is not the everyday path.

`lsp` is a multi-message stdio language server. Point your editor at `ooda lsp`.
Initialize advertises completion on `&`. `&Fs` completes to `&FsCap`.

`dump` prints the compiler view: tokens, AST, or check.

`outline` prints public signatures. `reflect` prints caps and the signature. Pass a symbol to focus one name. `--json` is the machine form.

`fix` applies common compiler patches. It asks yes or no unless you pass `--yes`. Set `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` so it can write.

`patch` replaces one function body. Give a relative path and a body file.

```
export OODA_FS_WRITEDIR="$PWD"
ooda fix hello.oo --yes
ooda patch hello.oo --replace-fn greet --with greet_body.oo
```

`context` counts and names `.oo` files in a path. Omit the path to use the current directory.

`digest` prints the sha256 of a file.

`health` prints liveness and whether `oodac/oodac` is present.

`pkg` prints the CAS lock inventory. `--json` is the machine form.

`bench` times `oodac check` and prints `ooda bench: check FILE Nms`.
`--run` also times `ooda run`.

## 3. Packages

`ooda add hello` installs from the stock store.

```
ooda add hello
ooda search hello
ooda registry
ooda registry add https://host
ooda registry remove https://host
ooda add hello --vendor ../registry
ooda pkg
```

`--vendor` is a folder on disk. `registry add` lists another HTTPS store. Search walks every listed store.

`ooda pm` is the same manager with extra verbs.

| Verb | What you get |
| :--- | :--- |
| `add` | Install a name into CAS and the lock. |
| `ingest` | Lock a local recipe directory. |
| `launch` | Re-check sha, caps, and yank. `--exec NAME` runs the gated payload. A marked untrusted body refuses. |
| `remove` | Drop a name from the lock. |
| `update` | Re-resolve from the vendor. |
| `outdated` | Compare the lock to the catalog. |
| `why` | Show who depends on a name. |
| `tree` | Print the dependency tree. |
| `vendor` | List names in the file vendor. |
| `clean` | Drop unused CAS dirs. |
| `audit` | Report yank, advisory, and pin hits. |
| `sbom` | Print a CycloneDX-lite object from the lock. |
| `pack` | Write an air-gap vendor tree. |

Many of these verbs take `--json`. See [Packages](Packages.oot) and [Capabilities](Safety.oot).

## 4. `oodac`

`oodac` is the compiler. `ooda` calls it.

```
oodac check <file.oo>
oodac check --json-errors <file.oo>
oodac emit-c <file.oo>
oodac emit-llvm <file.oo>
oodac emit-wasm <file.oo>
oodac emit-x86 <file.oo>
oodac --backend llvm emit-llvm <file.oo>
```

`emit-c` is the everyday emit. `ooda run` is check + emit C + `gcc` + exec.

`emit-llvm` writes LLVM IR. You can also pass `ooda build --target llvm` or `ooda build --emit-llvm`. `--release` is valid with LLVM. LLVM as a gcc replacement is residual.

`emit-wasm` writes a wasm module. It is an extra emit, not the everyday path.

`emit-x86` writes standalone x86_64 assembly with `_start`. Assemble with `as` and `ld`.

## 5. Environment

| Name | What it does |
| :--- | :--- |
| `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` | Directory `write_file` may write. |
| `OODA_TEST` | Set by `ooda test`. |

`env_get` sees `OODA_*` and `OO_*` keys. You pass `&EnvCap` to read them.

`ooda test` sets `OODA_TEST` for you. You set `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` when a command writes a file.

## 6. Permission tokens

A function that does an effect takes a token argument, for example `&FsReadCap`. Prefer the small token.

| Token | Lets the function |
| :--- | :--- |
| `&FsReadCap` | `read_file`, `path_exists`, `file_size` |
| `&FsWriteCap` | `write_file` (set `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`) |
| `&ProcessCap` | `process_exec`, `spawn`, `wait`, `kill` |
| `&NetCap` / `&HttpCap` / `&TcpCap` / `&UdpCap` | network ops |
| `&EnvCap` | `env_get` for `OODA_*` / `OO_*` |
| `&AllocCap` | heap and bump arenas |
| `&ThreadCap` | threads, channels, actor restart |
| `&SysCap` | Landlock jail, `setrlimit` |

Take `&FsReadCap` when you only read. Take `&HttpCap` when you only fetch. Take `&SysCap` when you need Landlock or `setrlimit`.
`&GpuCap` CPU/noop launch is product. Device GPU dispatch is residual.

On Linux, `&SysCap` can apply a Landlock jail and POSIX `setrlimit`. See [Capabilities](Safety.oot).

